From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 12:02:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: fail close-time drains on no progress, not a
 fixed total

A live GEM close waited at most 12 s in total for all of its VM's
pending fences, and file teardown likewise per VM. Under several
parallel heavy clients, a VM with 32 slow but retiring Works needs
longer than that: the GL CTS (2026-10-23, batch 0330) hit the close
timeout although the work retired later. The VM was marked broken,
and an exit-time timeout would have leaked the client's pinned pages
until reboot.

Drains now fail only when none of the VM's own fences has signaled for
the budget (12 s for closes, 35 s for VM unbind and admission), with a
120 s overall cap. A stuck or starved VM still fails within one budget.
The policy lives in g16_drain.rs and has host tests
(selftests/drivers/gpu/asahi/test-drain-progress.py). Drains re-check the
whole fence set at least once a second.

The close and exit timeout messages now report the wait, how many of the
VM's fences signaled during it, how many are still pending and how long
none has. "Retired 0" notification-poll warnings, which mean slow work
(the retirement watchdog reports hangs), are limited to one per 10 s with
a suppressed count.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
